findings.message:
Removing the `scripts/ao-update.sh` check makes the `git` classification rely solely on a `.git` directory four levels up from `import.meta.url`. Any vendored copy of the built CLI dropped inside another git repo (sub-tree, build artifact checked into a parent monorepo, symlinked workspace) now gets classified as a `git` install. `ao update` will then try to run `git pull && pnpm install && pnpm build && npm link` against the *enclosing* repo.

**Suggested fix:** combine the `.git` check with a stable AO marker, e.g.:

```ts
const aoPkgPath = resolve(repoRoot, "packages/ao/package.json");
if (existsSync(resolve(repoRoot, ".git")) && existsSync(aoPkgPath)) {
  try {
    const pkg = JSON.parse(readFileSync(aoPkgPath, "utf8"));
    if (pkg.name === "@aoagents/ao") return "git";
  } catch { /* fall through */ }
}
```

This brings back the same protection the removed `scripts/ao-update.sh` check used to give, just anchored to a file the PR no longer moves.

`AO_REPO_ROOT` is read with no validation and propagated into the spawned shell scripts (line 69). Combined with `ao-update.sh` doing `cd "$REPO_ROOT" && git pull && pnpm install && pnpm build && (cd packages/ao && npm link)` and `ao-doctor.sh --fix` doing `mkdir -p` plus `npm link` against `$REPO_ROOT`, an inherited or attacker-set `AO_REPO_ROOT` redirects all of those mutations at an arbitrary directory. Paths are quoted so this isn't shell injection, but it's still off-target filesystem mutation triggered by an env var.

**Suggested fix:** validate the override points at an actual AO checkout before trusting it, e.g.:

```ts
export function resolveRepoRoot(): string {
  const override = process.env["AO_REPO_ROOT"];
  if (!override) return DEFAULT_REPO_ROOT;
  const resolved = resolve(override);
  const marker = resolve(resolved, "packages/ao/package.json");
  if (!existsSync(marker)) {
    throw new Error(`AO_REPO_ROOT=${override} does not look like an agent-orchestrator checkout`);
  }
  return resolved;
}
```

Or drop the env override entirely if nothing legitimate needs it.

`AO_SCRIPT_LAYOUT` is caller-controlled with no provenance check. Setting it to `source-checkout` on a package install forces doctor to enforce repo-only checks (`pnpm` required, launcher checks, `npm link` remediation under `--fix`) against whatever `AO_REPO_ROOT` resolves to. Setting it to `package-install` on a source checkout downgrades `pnpm` from required to optional and skips launcher verification, hiding real install drift.

**Suggested fix:** only honor the override behind a deliberate dev opt-in, e.g.:

```ts
if (process.env["AO_DEV"] === "1" && (override === "package-install" || override === "source-checkout")) {
  return override;
}
return DEFAULT_SCRIPT_LAYOUT;
```

TOCTOU between this `hasRepoScript()` and `resolveScriptPath()` inside `executeScriptCommand` (script-runner.ts:46). If the script disappears between the two calls (concurrent `pnpm clean`, partial reinstall, cache eviction), the fallback is bypassed and the user sees a raw `Script not found: ao-update.sh` thrown error instead of the friendly fallback.

**Suggested fix:** drop the pre-check and wrap the call in `try/catch`, applying the fallback when `existsSync` fails inside `resolveScriptPath`:

```ts
try {
  await executeScriptCommand("ao-update.sh", args);
  invalidateCache();
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof Error && err.message.startsWith("Script not found")) {
    // ...same fallback as above (after the validation fix)
    return;
  }
  throw err;
}
```
